.\" Manual page for uxsm. Edit this .in file, never the installed page: the
.\" build fills in the version and the binary directory. `make check` lints it.
.\"
.\" Sections marked TODO are the ones still to write. Keep this page in step
.\" with `uxsm <command> -h`, which is where its wording comes from, and with
.\" docs/architecture.md, which explains the same things at length.
.TH UXSM 1 "2026-09-26" "uxsm @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uxsm \- X11 session manager for systemd \-\-user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uxsm
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uxsm
starts an X11 session as a set of
.BR "systemd \-\-user" (1)
units: the desktop becomes the main process of a service, the session gets an
environment prepared on purpose, and the standard graphical session targets are
pulled in. When the desktop exits, or the process that opened the session goes
away, systemd stops the whole set and uxsm restores the previous environment.
It is the X11 counterpart of
.BR uwsm (1).
.\" TODO: what a session looks like once it is up, and what it does not do.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Start an X11 session from a session entry or a command.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the running session.
.TP
.B finalize
Tell uxsm, from the desktop, that the session is ready.
.TP
.B app
Run an application in its own unit, inside the session.
.TP
.B entry
Generate a session entry and install it.
.TP
.B check
Check that the system is ready for uxsm, or whether a session is running.
.TP
.B setup
Change the system so that uxsm works fully.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.PP
Each command takes
.B \-h
for its own options.
.\" TODO: one subsection per command with its options and arguments, in the
.\" order above. Take the wording from `uxsm <command> -h`.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.\" TODO: XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP, XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP, XDG_SESSION_TYPE,
.\" XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, DISPLAY, and the uxsm/env* files.
.SH FILES
.\" TODO: the session entries directory, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/uxsm, the unit
.\" templates in @BINDIR@'s sibling lib/systemd/user, and the environment files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
